# NZ market-rate collection, once daily at 07:30 local.
#
#   crontab -e   # then paste, adjusting the two paths
#
# The workflow script takes its own single-instance lock, so an overrunning
# browser-rendered collection cannot interleave with the next schedule.
#
# --product defaults to home_loan. Term deposits are a SEPARATE market and
# need their own scheduled invocation -- see the second line below. Each
# invocation's --lock-file and --summary-file must be distinct per product:
# sharing either would make the second product's run either get skipped
# (EXIT_ALREADY_RUNNING) or silently overwrite the other product's summary.
#
# --lock-file below matches exactly what orchestration.default_lock_file_path()
# itself computes for --config configs/base.yaml's (unconfigured, so default)
# database path -- explicit here rather than relying on that default so an
# interactive `pricing-intelligence run` against the same database always
# shares this same lock, not a merely equal-looking one computed
# independently. If you configure a different pricing_intelligence.database_path,
# update these two paths to match (or drop --lock-file entirely and let both
# invocations compute the same default from that path instead).
#
# Exit codes:
#   0  every selected bank collected
#   1  no bank collected -- the run produced nothing
#   2  partial: at least one bank failed, market view incomplete
#   3  skipped, another collection was already running
#
# The subshell below logs a nonzero exit to collect.err.log for visibility,
# then re-exits with that same code -- replace the echo with whatever
# alerting you use. A plain `cmd || echo ...` would not do: echo itself
# always succeeds, so the overall cron command would exit 0 even when the
# collector failed, and both cron's own failure mail and any wrapper or
# monitoring that inspects the job's exit status would see a clean run.
#
# --config configs/base.yaml is passed explicitly: without it, the workflow
# falls back to PricingIntelligenceConfig() defaults and silently ignores any
# pricing_intelligence block (selected banks, validation thresholds,
# collection delays, database path) configured there.
#
# Optional alert delivery: to push detected rate moves to Slack, Teams, or
# Discord, set PRICING_INTELLIGENCE_ALERT_WEBHOOK_URL. Unlike launchd, cron
# lines below inherit a plain VAR=value assignment placed above them in the
# same crontab, e.g.:
#
#   PRICING_INTELLIGENCE_ALERT_WEBHOOK_URL=https://hooks.slack.com/services/T000/B000/xxxx
#
# Telegram delivery, independent of and stackable with the webhook above:
#
#   TELEGRAM_BOT_TOKEN=123456:AAAA-your-bot-token
#   TELEGRAM_CHAT_ID=-1001234567890
#
# Keep those lines out of any copy of this file under version control; only
# your installed crontab should carry real values. Unset is the default (no
# delivery), and a broken channel never fails the collection run.
#
# CRON_TZ pins every schedule below to Pacific/Auckland explicitly, regardless
# of the host machine's own system timezone -- vixie-cron 4.0+, cronie, and
# macOS's built-in cron (a vixie-cron 4.1 descendant) all read CRON_TZ to
# decide which timezone "30 7" is evaluated in. A plain TZ= line does NOT do
# this: cron(8)/crontab(5) only pass TZ through as an environment variable
# for the launched command's own use -- it has no effect on when the job
# actually fires. Without CRON_TZ, "07:30" silently means the machine's local
# time, which is wrong the moment this runs on a host not already set to NZ
# time. (The launched command itself already resolves NZ-local time
# explicitly via zoneinfo where it matters -- e.g. cli.py's _NZ_TIMEZONE --
# so it does not depend on TZ being set in its environment either.)
CRON_TZ=Pacific/Auckland

30 7 * * * cd /path/to/ai-bank-pricing-engine && mkdir -p data/processed/pricing_intelligence && ( .venv/bin/python3 scripts/collect_market_rates_workflow.py --config configs/base.yaml --render-javascript --quiet --lock-file data/processed/pricing_intelligence/collection-home-loan-observations.lock --summary-file data/processed/pricing_intelligence/last_run_home_loan.json >> data/processed/pricing_intelligence/collect.log 2>&1; code=$?; [ "$code" -eq 0 ] || echo "NZ home-loan rate collection exited $code $(date)" >> data/processed/pricing_intelligence/collect.err.log; exit "$code" )

35 7 * * * cd /path/to/ai-bank-pricing-engine && mkdir -p data/processed/pricing_intelligence && ( .venv/bin/python3 scripts/collect_market_rates_workflow.py --config configs/base.yaml --product term_deposit --render-javascript --quiet --lock-file data/processed/pricing_intelligence/collection-term-deposit-observations.lock --summary-file data/processed/pricing_intelligence/last_run_term_deposit.json >> data/processed/pricing_intelligence/collect.log 2>&1; code=$?; [ "$code" -eq 0 ] || echo "NZ term-deposit rate collection exited $code $(date)" >> data/processed/pricing_intelligence/collect.err.log; exit "$code" )

# A daily brief right after the morning run, and an optional second
# collection later in the day, are both opt-in operator choices -- see
# "Daily workflow" and "Scheduled run" in docs/pricing_intelligence.md's
# Operations section for ready-to-adapt schedule lines.
